  title: "Śrī Varasiddhi Vināyaka Vratam",
  blurb: "The worship kept on Vināyaka Cavitī, read from while it is performed. The breath is installed in the clay, so the god is present in it for as long as the worship lasts. Then the sixteen services, and the three counted offerings that give this vrata its shape — twenty-one leaves, twenty-one flowers, twenty-one pairs of dūrvā, each offered under a name of its own. The hundred and eight names are here in full. It closes with the kathā of how Gaṇeśa became lord of obstacles, and why the moon is not looked at on this night.",
  about: "Smārta paddhati of the Telugu country, in the Devanāgarī edition of vignanam.org, collated against the Vināyaka Vrata Kalpam of stotranidhi and, for the two stotras it shares, against Gītā Press. Where the two differ — the aṅga-pūjā lists, the names attached to the leaves, the form of the jewel's name — neither has been merged into the other. The kathā is printed by both in prose, not in Sanskrit, and none has been composed to fill the gap. The direction that goes with each unit is given in English only, as the source gives it; the mantra's meaning is in Telugu. Vrata procedure differs by family and by sampradāya. Where your elders differ, they decide.",
